    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "\"This CD-ROM contains digitized images of coral cores taken during the summer of 1986 from large colonies of Montastrea faveolata in Biscayne National Park, roughly 12 km south of Miami on the Atlantic side of Florida. The collection of coral cores and slabs is stored at the U.S. Geological Survey's Center for Coastal Geology and Regional Marine Studies located in St. Petersburg, Florida. The digitized images of coral growth bands along with their age assignments provide the basic data from which coral growth rates and general reef conditions over the past two and a half centuries may be determined. The digitized image of fluorescent growth bands is designed to be used with the x-ray images of the same cores. This collection is intended for use in further paleoclimatic and paleoenvironmental research focusing on the upper Florida Keys. As researchers become more adept at interpreting proxy data from coral skeletons, this collection may provide information about annual and seasonal environmental parameters such as water temperature, regional runoff, upwelling conditions, records from Biscayne National Park, Florida.\"",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Tao, Yucong and Roulier, L M and Higgins, K M and Hayes, M L and Halley, R B and Hudson, J H. (1999). USGS OFR 99-340: Digitized images of coral growth bands, fluorescent growth bands, and their age assignments from cores of Montastrea faveolata, Biscayne National Park, upper Florida Keys. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: https://purl.stanford.edu/mm479wg2237",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "displayLabel": "Preferred Citation",
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
